%S A398235 491923,491951,491969,491983,664036843,664036853,664036859,664036963,
%T A398235 664037173,664037177,664037203,664037207,664037243,664037281,
%U A398235 664037321,664037383,664037443,664037971,664037993,664037999,664038017,664038047,664038217,664038257,664038301
%N A398235 Primes p for which pi_{24;7,13}(p) - pi_{24;1,19}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A398235 From _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_: (Start)
%C A398235 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,19} and S = {7,13}, corresponding to T = {chi_{-8}, chi_24}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A398235 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_{-8}(q) + chi_24(q))/2 = 1.
%C A398235 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;7,13}(x) > pi_{24;1,19}(x) has logarithmic density 0.98573, making the race between {1,19} and {7,13} the 15th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint below.
%C A398235 Let D(x) = pi_{24;7,13}(x) - pi_{24;1,19}(x). Below x0 = 7*10^12 there are respectively 30659637567, 30659688364, 30659698140, 30659716342, 30659718072, 30659718286, 30659752652, and 30659759379 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 25993. There are 9 sign-changing zones giving a total of 976399 terms below x0:
%C A398235   (1) a(1..4) = 491923-491983 (there are 5 primes a(1) <= p <= a(4) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -2 at p = 491977);
%C A398235   (2) a(5..439) = 664036843-664462427 (there are 7642 primes a(5) <= p <= a(439) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -40 at p = 664098499);
%C A398235   (3) a(440..1561) = 1090244761-1109716963 (there are 21082 primes a(440) <= p <= a(1561) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -59 at p = 1109592073);
%C A398235   (4) a(1562..17879) = 4770257923-5337736223 (there are 13576581 primes a(1562) <= p <= a(17879) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -875 at p = 5149139209);
%C A398235   (5) a(17880..35725) = 67117741993-67618160837 (there are 6352856 primes a(17880) <= p <= a(35725) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -960 at p = 67463579971);
%C A398235   (6) a(35726..42440) = 480151933441-480313346399 (there are 4196361 primes a(35726) <= p <= a(42440) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -571 at p = 480243843073);
%C A398235   (7) a(42441..137107) = 1054596158857-1096646819221 (there are 146923127 primes a(42441) <= p <= a(137107) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -3892 at p = 1093234321099);
%C A398235   (8) a(137108..913898) = 5302443127537-5811530206277 (there are 7771601510 primes a(137108) <= p <= a(913898) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -17561 at p = 5492793722953);
%C A398235   (9) a(913899..976399) = 6200300153833-6213572163499 (there are 215974678 primes a(913899) <= p <= a(976399) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -8853 at p = 6201941192851).
%C A398235 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 8158653842. (End)
%H A398235 Jianing Song, <a href="/A398235/b398235.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..42440</a> (the first 6 sign-changing zones)
%H A398235 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A398235 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A398235/a398235.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500000</a>, <a href="/A398235/a398235_1.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 500001..976399</a> (all terms below 7*10^12).
%H A398235 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A398235 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A398235 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A398235 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A398235 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==19, k--); if(p%24==7||p%24==13, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
